<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Armenian SSR</h1><p class="st-meta">1920 CE – 1991 CE · capital Yerevan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Soviet Armenia, a republic of the USSR from 1920 to 1991, which saw rapid industrialisation, mass immigration and the devastating 1988 earthquake.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In December 1920, with Turkish forces in the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> in the east, the government of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-republic-of-armenia">First Republic of Armenia</a> ceded power to the Bolsheviks. After a brief anti-Bolshevik revolt in 1921, the Armenian SSR was established, joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/transcaucasian-sfsr">Transcaucasian SFSR</a> in 1922 and became a full union republic in 1936.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Soviet rule brought industry, hydroelectric power at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-sevan">Lake Sevan</a>, universities and the growth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a> to a planned capital designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-tamanian">Alexander Tamanian</a>. Tens of thousands of diaspora <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> resettled there after 1946. The church was repressed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Stalin</a>'s purges struck the intelligentsia. In 1965 mass demonstrations marked the 50th anniversary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1988 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh-movement">Karabakh movement</a> mobilised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> to demand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1988-armenian-earthquake">Spitak earthquake</a> killed about 25,000 people. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a> voted for independence in September 1991, becoming the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-republic-of-armenia">Republic of Armenia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/armenian-ssr.jpg" alt="Armenian SSR" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Aivazovsky and others · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Armenian_Soviet_Socialist_Republic_(1952%E2%80%931990).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1920 CE – 1991 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yerevan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Alexander Myasnikian, Levon Ter-Petrosyan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Armenian Apostolic Christianity (officially atheist state)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Armenian; Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/armenia">Armenia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Armenian Soviet Socialist Republic, Soviet Armenia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1956&amp;focus=armenian-ssr" aria-label="Armenian SSR on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5775 741 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Armenian SSR lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6095 997L6144 1018M6095 997L6095 997M6095 997L6090 947M6095 997L5820 533M6095 997L6095 997"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6144 1018h0M6095 997h0M6090 947h0M5820 533h0M6095 997h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6095 997h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6095 997h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Yerevan. <a href="/atlas?year=1956&amp;focus=armenian-ssr">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
